water: 
change log. From 09/09/2015. Most recent changes first / on top.
Guillermo OLMEDO
-----------------------------------------------------------------
VERSION 1.0

Fixes that change results:

* waterOptions(writeResults = TRUE) gave wrong results: every function
  wrote and overwrote a file with a fixed name, so later calls changed the
  objects that read it (e.g. a "Perrier" METRIC.EB() run used the
  short.crops roughness, mean LE 194.8 instead of 226.9 W/m2), and two runs
  overwrote each other's EB.tif (#99).

* Reference ET follows ASCE-EWRI (2005) (#89): Rs/Rso is limited to 0.3-1
  (it was set to 0 below 0.3), the cloudiness function of the last sunny
  period is carried over the night (it was fixed at 0.05), Cd and G switch
  between day and night on Rn > 0 (it was Rs > 0), the extraterrestrial
  radiation is integrated between sunrise and sunset, and T + 273 is used in
  the aerodynamic term. dailyET() sums the hourly periods ending at 01:00 to
  24:00, as ASCE does (it summed those ending at 00:00 to 23:00). Daily ETr:
  Talca example 7.09 -> 6.53 mm (-7.8%), Mendoza 5.34 -> 5.41 mm (+1.3%);
  daily ET (ET24h()) changes in the same proportion. The instantaneous ETr
  at the overpass changes by less than 0.02%. Checked against the hourly
  test case of the RefET package (ASCE method) within 1e-5 mm/h.
* dailyET() accepts a Date for date, and returns NA with a warning when the
  records do not cover the day (it summed the hours available) (#90).
* Anchor pixels (#92): new default anchors.method = "percentile", with
  criteria relative to the scene, in the spirit of the automated
  calibration of Allen et al. (2013) and Morton et al. (2013): cold
  candidates have NDVI at or above its 95th percentile and LAI of at least
  4; hot candidates NDVI at or below its 10th percentile, NDVI above 0 and
  albedo 0.10 to 0.35 (no water, urban surfaces or bright rock); both with
  a homogeneous surface temperature (sd up to 1.5 K in 5 x 5 pixels; NDVI
  quantiles over land, so water does not move them); the
  coldest and hottest 20% are the anchors, averaged by calcH(). With the
  fixed windows of "flexible" (n = 5), the Landsat 8 example had ETrF >
  1.1 on 15.8% of the pixels (cold anchors warmer than many crops); with
  "percentile", 0.6%; mean LE 255.5 -> 223.6 W/m2 (-12.5%), daily ET 4.13 -> 3.62 mm;
  Landsat 7 example mean LE -5.6%. The other methods are unchanged; use
  anchors.method = "flexible" for the old default. Parameters in
  anchors.control().
* Land surface temperature (#95): LST.method = "auto" (new default) uses
  the Level-2 surface temperature (image.SR$Band_10) for Landsat 8 and 9
  when it is present, also when image.DN is passed (before, Ts was
  estimated from the thermal band with "SC", +3 K against the Level-2
  product in the example); "L2" requires it. The thermal-band path is now
  "METRIC" (Allen et al. 2007, Eqs. 20-21) with the paper's general
  atmospheric values (tau 0.866, Rp 0.91, Rsky 1.32), or per-pixel values
  with surfaceTemperature(atmosphere = ); for Landsat 8/9 it warns that the
  general values can bias Ts by several K (-6 K in the example). "SC" was
  removed; "SW" is deprecated. Landsat 7 example: mean Ts 313.9 -> 303.7 K
  (air 295.7 K), mean LE +0.8% (the calibration absorbs most of a uniform
  bias in Ts).
* calcH() (#94): when the wind extrapolated to 200 m is below 2 m/s, 2 m/s
  is used (calcH.control(u200.min = ); before, below 1 m/s it jumped to
  4 m/s, so 0.50 and 0.53 m/s at the station gave very different results);
  momentumRoughnessLength() (and so METRIC.EB()) keeps the momentum
  roughness length at least 0.005 m (bare soil, Allen et al. 2007), for
  every method (a Z.om passed to calcH() is used as given); maxit is the
  number of iterations (one fewer were run); the iteration stops with an
  error when it diverges. Optional
  damping: calcH.control(damping = 0.5). calcH() and METRIC.EB()
  ($calibration) report a, b, converged, iterations and u200; a negative
  friction velocity (very unstable air over tall roughness) gives NA instead
  of a flipped H; method = "lm" warns with more than 10 anchor pairs. Mean LE:
  Landsat 8 example -1.6% (roughness floor), Landsat 7 example +0.9%.
* calcH(): the ET of the cold pixels was ETp.coef times the hourly grass
  reference ET (ETo), while ET24h() and dailyET() extrapolate with the
  alfalfa reference ETr. Both now use ETr, as in Allen et al. (2007), so the
  default ETp.coef = 1.05 is the paper's 1.05 * ETr. With the same ETp.coef,
  ET increases by the ETr/ETo ratio (11% and 15% in the examples). Scripts
  that used ETp.coef = 1.2 to approximate 1.05 * ETr with ETo should now use
  1.05; the vignettes and examples now use the default. With
  anchors.method = "flexible", the grass-based Kc of Gonzalez et al. (2015)
  is multiplied by ETo/ETr, so that update gives the same cold-pixel ET as
  before.
* Z.om.ws (momentum roughness length of the weather station, Allen et al.
  2007, Eq. 32): the default is now 0.015 m, 0.123 times the 0.12 m clipped
  grass of a standard station (Allen et al. 1998), in METRIC.EB() (it was
  0.0018) and calcH() (it was 0.03). METRIC is not very sensitive to it
  (0.4% in LE in the example).

* METRICtopo(): since the migration to terra, slope and aspect were
  computed in degrees and used as radians, so every run with a DEM
  (plain = FALSE) was wrong (e.g. negative net radiation). With
  plain = TRUE the results no longer have an NA border.
* solarAngles(): the hour angle is now negative before solar noon; east and
  west slopes were swapped for morning (Landsat) overpasses (plain = FALSE).
* calcTOAr(): Landsat 8/9 TOA reflectance was divided by the incidence angle
  instead of its cosine (about 22% too high), which biased LAI and the anchor
  pixels whenever image.DN was used.
* calcTOAr(), calcRadiance(): bands are taken by name. The L7_Talca example
  stores SWIR2 after Thermal1, so "SWIR2" was computed from the thermal band.
* read.WSdata(): the overpass time in the MTL file (UTC) was read as
  Europe/London time, one hour off for scenes acquired during British Summer
  Time (late March to late October).
* calcAnchors(): pixels with masked reflectance (e.g. clouds) could be
  selected as anchors; "best" failed to find cold pixels; all methods
  returned only one hot and one cold pixel regardless of n; "random" could
  select a pixel that was not a candidate.
* LAI(method = "metric"): LAI is 6 for SAVI > 0.687 (Allen et al. 2007);
  dense vegetation gave NaN.
* calcRadiance(): Landsat 7 radiance was NaN (MTL values without "E-"
  notation), which broke LAI.method = "vineyard" for Landsat 7.
* LAI(method = "turner"): Turner et al. (1999) predict NDVI from LAI, but the
  equation was applied as LAI = f(NDVI), so LAI never exceeded 0.75 and
  METRIC.EB() found no cold anchors. It is now inverted (LAI 0 to 6) (#70).
* hourlyET(), dailyET(): the solar time correction used the station
  longitude instead of the centre of its time zone, so it was always 0.
  long.z now defaults to 15 degrees per hour of the UTC offset of the
  station records (-45 for UTC-3), including daylight saving time; hourly
  ETr at the overpass of the Talca example: -2.4% (#77). ET24h() now uses
  the instantaneous ETr at the overpass, as calcH() does (ETrF =
  instantaneous ET / instantaneous ETr).
* albedo(): for MODIS only 6 of the 7 coefficients were used (#78).
* loadImageSR() masks pixels where any band is outside the valid range of
  the product (7273 to 43636 DN, reflectance 0 to 1), not only the fill
  value, in every layer (surface temperature included), and says how many.
  In the Landsat 8 example, 109 pixels (0.4%) were negative and raised the
  maximum NDVI from 0.923 to 1.88, which moved the NDVI threshold of the
  cold anchors: the cold anchors change, and mean LE and daily ET increase
  by 4.2% (4.4% on the cells valid before and after). Some masked pixels are
  dense crops with slightly negative blue reflectance (an atmospheric
  over-correction of the product), so the outputs have small holes over
  some of the best-watered fields. loadImage() sets the Level-1 fill (DN 0)
  to NA: it gave Ts = -53.6 K and entered the anchor search; for MODIS, the
  land surface temperature fill (0) is set to NA (#85).
* albedo(), LAI(), soilHeatFlux(), calcSR() and calcAnchors() select the
  bands by name, as calcTOAr() and calcRadiance() did: with the bands in
  another order, LAI and albedo were silently wrong. For MODIS stacks (R
  and NIR first), LAI() and soilHeatFlux() used the blue and green bands
  for NDVI; albedo(sat = "auto") recognises MODIS by its SWIR3 layer (#86).
* Example data: the wind speed in apples.csv is in km/h, but the
  read.WSdata() example and the METRIC vignettes read it as m/s. The daily
  reference ET of the example was 10.2 instead of 7.0 mm, and the daily ET
  of the vignettes about 45% too high (#72). The example files and their
  units are documented in ?`water-extdata`.

New:

* ET24h(EB) takes the result of METRIC.EB() and returns layers ETinst
  (mm/h), ETrF (reference ET fraction, the alfalfa-basis crop coefficient),
  ET24 (mm/day) and HgtRnG (pixels where H > Rn - G, where ETinst and ETrF
  are negative). ETr.daily defaults to dailyET() for the day of the
  overpass. The help page explains how to compare ETrF with a grass-basis
  Kc. The call with Rn, G, H and Ts gives the same ET24 as ET24h(EB) (#91).
* Single-layer results are named (e.g. "Rn", "G", "H", "albedo", "Z.om",
  "ET24"); they had empty names (#91).
* Mountain mode, plain = FALSE (#97): new Crad(), the slope correction of
  daily ET (Allen et al. 2007, Eqs. 55-57), used by ET24h() for METRIC.EB()
  results computed with a DEM (it was never computed); the anchors are
  chosen on Ts referred to the elevation of the station, as the dT
  calibration; slopes facing away from the sun get no direct radiation (it
  could be negative); the lapse rate is calcH.control(lapse = ). Optional:
  calcH.control(slope.min1 = TRUE) keeps the terrain factor of the
  roughness (Eq. 35) at least 1, as in the METRIC manual. Landsat 7 with
  DEM_Talca: C_rad 0.90 to 1.20 (1st to 99th percentile; limited to 0.5-2,
  with the cosine of the incidence angle at least 0.1), mean LE -0.9%,
  mean daily ET -0.5%. Flat runs are unchanged.
* Hot and cold anchors (#96, optional): calcH.control(ETrF.hot = ) sets
  the ET of the hot pixels after rain (Allen et al. 2007, Eq. 47; default
  0, dry soil), and METRIC.EB() warns when the station recorded more than
  5 mm of rain in the 3 days before the image. calcH.control(
  cold.NDVI.rule = TRUE) lowers the ET fraction of cold anchors with NDVI
  below 0.8 for every automatic method (before, only "flexible" with
  relaxed criteria); off by default.
* Calibration guards (#93): calcAnchors() warns when there are fewer than
  20 hot or cold candidates (anchors.control(min.candidates = )): the area
  may be too small or too homogeneous; it returns the candidate counts and,
  for "flexible", the relaxed criteria of each type (the flex column now has
  the hot relaxation on the hot rows). METRIC.EB() gives a message when the
  scene has clouds (CLOUD_COVER) and the image was not masked with
  cfmask(), and keeps the counts in $calibration.
* Calibration diagnostics (#98): METRIC.EB() returns in $calibration the
  mean Ts of the hot and cold anchors and their difference, the quantiles
  of ETrF at the overpass and the shares of pixels with ETrF > 1.1,
  ETrF < 0 and H > Rn - G, with the iteration results; it gives a one-line
  message, and a warning when ETrF > 1.1 on more than 10% of the pixels
  (cold anchors too warm; 15.8% in the Landsat 8 example with "flexible").
  New summary() method and plotAnchors() (anchors over a false-colour
  composite or Ts); print() includes the summary.
* Consistent arguments (#102): METRIC.Rn() and METRIC.G() take their
  arguments in the order of METRIC.EB() (image.DN, image.SR,
  WeatherStation, MTL, ...); LAI(image, method), albedo(image.SR, coeff,
  sat, aoi) and calcH(anchors, Ts, ..., method) take the data first (old
  positional calls of LAI() and albedo() work, with a warning; the others
  stop with a clear error); unused aoi arguments are last and warn when
  passed; METRIC.EB(), METRIC.Rn() and METRIC.G() check that image.SR,
  image.DN, surf.temp, thermalband and DEM are on the same grid. New
  default alb.coeff = "auto": "Olmedo" for Landsat 8 and 9, "Tasumi" for
  Landsat 7, "Liang" for MODIS (Landsat 8/9 runs that relied on the old
  default "Tasumi" change by about 0.6% in LE).
* anchors.control() and calcH.control() group the parameters of the anchor
  selection and of the H iteration: METRIC.EB(anchors.control = ,
  calcH.control = ). deltaTemp, minDist and WSbuffer in extraParameters
  still work, with a deprecation message (#102).

Landsat 8 and 9 (Collection 2):

* METRIC.EB(), METRIC.Rn() and METRIC.G() run from Collection 2 Level-2
  products: image.DN is optional when image.SR is supplied, and surf.temp
  takes the Level-2 surface temperature. (Francisco Martín Corvalán)
* loadImageSR() reads Collection 2 Level-2 files (SR_B2 to SR_B7, plus ST_B10
  as "Band_10" in Kelvin), and stops if any of the six reflectance bands is
  missing. The retired ESPA format is no longer read (#74); the Landsat 8
  vignette points to the download guides for USGS and Planetary Computer.
* cfmask() masks clouds with the Collection 2 QA_PIXEL band (fill, dilated
  cloud, cirrus, cloud and cloud shadow bits), looks for the band in path,
  and implements the buffer argument (#55, #47).
* Landsat 9 support: getSat() returns "L9", L9 is handled like L8 (same
  bands), and surfaceTemperature() reads the thermal calibration constants
  from the MTL file (new MTL argument, required for L9).
* sat = "auto" detects the satellite from the MTL file or the file names.
* New example data: a 134 x 184 pixel Collection 2 Level-2 crop (SR, ST,
  QA_PIXEL and MTL) in inst/extdata/L8_C2. The ESPA surface reflectance
  example files were removed.
* The Landsat 8 vignette is now an evaluated Landsat 8/9 Collection 2
  workflow and no longer refers to the retired ESPA service (#52).

Other changes and fixes:

* calcAnchors(): works when the weather station is outside the image;
  "flexible" relaxes the criteria near the station and then, with a
  warning, in the whole image; failed selections explain what to check;
  plots = TRUE works without a weather station (#46).
* read.WSdata(): clear errors for unknown time zones, unreadable dates and
  non-numeric columns; rows with unreadable dates are removed with a
  warning; tibbles and integer columns work (#41). An overpass outside the
  station records gives a warning instead of an error (#28).
  read.WSdata2() works (it reads with read.csv2()).
* MTL must be the file name, not its contents (#45).
* loadImage() accepts any scene prefix, e.g. real-time tier or renamed
  files, and explains the expected names when nothing matches (#50).
* prepareSRTMdata() returns the DEM on the image grid (#31).
* New getDEM(): downloads the Copernicus DEM GLO-30 (30 m) tiles that cover
  the image from the AWS Open Data registry (no account needed, only the
  image area is read) and returns the DEM on the image grid (#75).
  checkSRTMgrids() returns the file names of the SRTM tiles that
  prepareSRTMdata() reads, instead of outdated download links (#71).
* METRIC.G() can run: same arguments as METRIC.Rn(), plus G.method and an
  optional Rn. METRIC.EB(), METRIC.Rn() and METRIC.G() share the same
  surface computations, so Rn and G are identical across them; Rn and G
  gained L and LST.method.
* METRIC.EB(), METRIC.Rn(), METRIC.G(): aoi crops the inputs (an aoi in
  another CRS is reprojected); manual anchors work with the default
  anchors.method; Zom.method = "custom" works; a thermal layer named
  "thermal.low" is also found by METRIC.Rn() and METRIC.G().
* print() and writeRaster() work on METRIC.EB() results (writeRaster() is
  now an S4 method).
* waterOptions(outputFolder, SRTMrepo) work; waterWriteResults = TRUE no
  longer deletes temporary files in use.
* calcH(method = "lm") could loop forever.
* hourlyET() only accepts ET = "ETr" or "ETo" (calcH() requested the
  invalid "ETor" and silently got ETo).
* calcH() no longer fails with "raster has no values" when no pixel meets
  the stability condition.
* ET24h() plots with terra instead of spplot.
* Example data (L7_Talca, DEM_Talca) are terra PackedSpatRaster objects;
  use rast() to unpack them.
* albedo(sat = "auto") and calcSR(sat = "auto", MTL = ) no longer look for
  Landsat files in the working directory; mtlValues() stops for keys that
  Level-2 MTL files repeat in several groups unless a group is given;
  print() on weather station data returns it invisibly; LAI(sat = ) warns
  that sat is not used; typos in messages (#78).
* Help pages: references checked and completed with DOIs (#79); links
  between help pages; examples for METRIC.Rn(), METRIC.G() and
  loadImageSR() (#81).
* The Planetary Computer download guide only lists the scenes when ids is
  empty, and downloads the ones listed in ids (#80).
* Regression tests in tests/.

Input checks:

* Method arguments (sat, alb.coeff, LAI.method, LST.method, G.method,
  Zom.method, anchors.method in the METRIC functions; method, coeff, sat
  and ET in the others) must be one of the documented names, exactly. An
  unknown name stops before any raster work, with the valid names and a
  hint for typos (e.g. "did you mean \"Olmedo\"?"); before, it was ignored
  or gave an unrelated error later. LST.method = "SW" with Landsat 7 stops.
  METRIC.EB() checks extraParameters: a named numeric vector with known
  names, and a and b for Zom.method = "custom", fLAI and h for "Perrier"
  (#82).
* No hidden inputs (#87): aoi crops only when it is passed (an object called
  aoi in the workspace was used by loadImage(), loadImageSR() and albedo()),
  and the MTL file is never searched in the working directory (an unrelated
  MTL file there changed the results or stopped read.WSdata()). Functions
  that need it stop with "MTL is required", and sat = "auto" needs MTL
  (loadImage(sat = "auto") still reads the MTL file in its path folder,
  which defaults to the working directory).
  read.WSdata() without MTL says that the conditions at the overpass were
  not computed. dailyET() without MTL takes the day from date or from the
  overpass conditions (at.sat); it summed the first 24 records, another day
  in the examples. calcTOAr() and calcSR() no longer try to load or compute
  a missing image.
* read.WSdata() (#83): a named columns vector is matched by name, in any
  order (the names were ignored, so columns in another order were read as
  the wrong variables); records are sorted by time (reversed records
  failed, shuffled ones gave wrong overpass conditions), exact duplicates
  are removed and records with the same time and different values (e.g.
  the repeated hour when daylight saving time ends) are averaged, with a
  warning (many conflicting times outside one clock hour stop: a logger
  fault); values are checked after cf: impossible ones stop and unusual
  ones warn with the likely unit problem (Kelvin or Fahrenheit, RH as a
  fraction, wind in km/h, radiation in MJ); a semicolon-separated file
  points to read.WSdata2().
* Surface temperature must be in Kelvin (#86): METRIC.EB(), METRIC.Rn(),
  METRIC.G(), calcAnchors() and calcH() stop when it looks like Celsius
  (ET was 86% too high, with wrong Rn and G), and when more than 1% of the
  cells are below 200 K (unmasked fill or clouds); fewer such cells give a
  warning. A stack whose layers have the standard names (B, G, R, NIR,
  ...) but lacks one that is needed is an error; layers without standard
  names are used by position, with a warning.
* calcH() checks the anchors before iterating (#88): columns and labels
  ("Hot" and "Cold" are accepted), inside the image (sf anchors in another
  CRS are transformed), Ts, Rn, G, Z.om and DEM not NA there, and the hot
  anchors at least 5 K warmer than the cold ones (each pair for
  method = "lm"). Swapped labels ran without complaint (mean H 378 instead
  of 264 W/m2), and a smaller difference means an area without the wet
  and dry extremes METRIC needs (in a 600 m farm crop, daily ET was about
  40% low). The Landsat 8 example has 6.7 K.
* Weather at the overpass (#84): read.WSdata() warns when the station
  radiation at the overpass is below 80% (or above 110%) of the ASCE-EWRI
  clear-sky radiation: the cold pixels are calibrated with it, and METRIC
  needs a clear image. The Landsat 8 example now warns: 587 W/m2 against a
  clear-sky 860 W/m2. New options, off by default: timestamps = "end" or
  "start" for records that are means over a period (interpolated at the
  middle of the periods); overpass.radiation = "clear-sky" to calibrate
  with clear-sky radiation (calcH() and ET24h() use the same reference
  ET); Kt, the clearness of the atmosphere (it was fixed at 1), in
  METRIC.EB(), METRIC.Rn(), METRIC.G(), incSWradiation(), incLWradiation()
  and calcSR().

Behaviour changes to check in existing scripts:

* Method names are matched exactly: partial or differently capitalised
  names (e.g. ET = "ETR", G.method = "T") are errors (#82).
* LST.method = "SC" is an error (removed); the default is now "auto"
  (#95).
* METRIC.Rn() and METRIC.G(): arguments in the order of METRIC.EB(); name
  them in positional calls. LAI(), albedo(), calcH(), calcTOAr(), calcSR(),
  soilHeatFlux(), surfaceTemperature() and calcAnchors(): data first,
  method and aoi later. The default alb.coeff is "auto" (#102).
* The default anchors.method is now "percentile" (#92); pass
  anchors.method = "flexible" to reproduce earlier results. n is not used
  by "percentile".
* dailyET() sums the hourly periods ending at 01:00 to 24:00, and returns
  NA, with a warning, when the records do not cover the day (#89, #90).
* Single-layer results have names ("Rn", "G", "H", "ET24", ...): check
  scripts that select or rename layers (#91).
* Deprecated: deltaTemp, minDist and WSbuffer in extraParameters (use
  anchors.control()) and LST.method = "SW" (#102, #95).
* Pass MTL explicitly: it is no longer searched in the working directory;
  waterOptions(autoAoi) is ignored, with a warning: pass aoi instead (#87).
* dailyET(): the day is now taken in the time zone of the station records,
  for date and for the MTL acquisition date (both used the UTC day: a few
  hours off for date, and the previous day for stations east of about
  UTC+10); a day without 24 hourly values gives a warning (#87, #90).
* read.WSdata(): the names in columns are now used (they were ignored, and
  only the order counted): a warning is given when the names change the
  meaning of the vector. Check scripts whose names did not match the
  columns (#83).

* sf moved from Depends to Imports: library(water) no longer attaches sf,
  so call library(sf) to use its functions directly (#81).
* hourlyET(), dailyET(): see the long.z default above (#77).
* cfmask() now applies its buffer argument (60 m by default) around the
  masked clouds and shadows, also with keep; use buffer = 0 for the
  previous behaviour.
* read.WSdata(): a time zone that R does not know is now an error (R only
  warned and used UTC).
* hourlyET(): the solar time correction for long.z uses degrees, as in
  ASCE (before it was about 57 times too small); longitudes are negative
  for west.
* read.WSdata2(): the default cf is now c(1, 1, 1), as in read.WSdata().
* No side effects (#100): METRIC.EB(), METRIC.Rn(), METRIC.G(), calcAnchors(),
  calcH() and ET24h() plot only with plots = TRUE (default: only in
  interactive sessions), METRIC.EB() and plot() of weather station data
  restore par(), and progress output is given with message() (the anchors
  only with verbose = TRUE) instead of print(). ET24h() plots all the cells.
* waterOptions(writeResults = TRUE): only the final outputs (EB, ET24, DEM)
  are written to outputFolder; intermediate results go to terra's temporary
  files with unique names (#99).
* prepareSRTMdata(): with a polygon, the DEM is on the Landsat 30 m grid
  of its projected CRS; a longitude/latitude polygon is an error.
* Documentation: help files updated for terra/sf, Landsat 9 and
  Collection 2, value sections added (#19, #5); vignette fixes (#6).


VERSION 0.8.4

* Fixed read.WSdata example using a fixed (the correct one) timezone. Before this
  timezone of the example was estimated with user timezone
* Fixed mistmatch between LAI code and LAI documentation
* Suppress confusing warning on read.WSdata()


VERSION 0.8

* New method for anchors pixels selection named "flexible". This method
  will find the pixel with the best condition when there aren't any perfect 
  candidates. It will relax the conditions needed and then adjust the ETp.coef
  based on Gonzalez et al 2015. See calcH()
* Added a new method to G estimation. Now the method can be choosen from 
  soilHeatFlux() and also in METRIC.EB()

VERSION 0.7

* New S3 class for the results of METRIC.EB(). The new class includes the
  RasterStack with the LSEB results, the weather station data used, and the
  parameters and methods selected in METRIC.EB().
* When using METRIC.EB(), calcAnchors always uses method="short.crops" not 
  matter the method selected for Zom.method.
* water can handle filenames for Landsat images using the pre-collection or
  the collection 1 sets.
* New method for calcAnchors: "flexible". This method is based in the "best" 
  method, but, will relax the requesites for the anchors when there is no pixel
  with those conditions. "flexible" is the default method now.
* in LAI() the method "vineyard" used the toa reflectancy image. This was 
  corrected and now uses the radiance image
* Added a function to estimate radiance from a digital counts image and a 
  metadata file: calcRadiance().
* Several minor bugs fixed.


VERSION 0.6.1

* Added a workaround for read.WSdata() when there is not rain data
* Fixed a bug in momentumRoughness() when using the Perrier equation
* Added extraParameters to METRIC.EB. This allows to pass more parameters to 
  the internal functions and apply different methods. i.e. extra parameters for
  different Zom.methods.

VERSION 0.6

* Added a new function loadImageSR to load surface reflectance from
  Landsat 8 from ESPA files
* calcSR only works for L7
* calcTOAr() now estimates TOA reflectance for Landsat 8
* surfaceTemperature() uses the scene in DN and estimates the brightness
  temperature for Landsat 7 or 8. Argument thermalband is deprecated.
* Added a new example of weather station for the new Landsat 8 example
* Added a Landsa 8 scene as example dataset in raw format complete with the
  surface reflectance estimated by ESPA
* Added a new vignette describing how to use Landsat 8 data in water package

VERSION 0.5.1

* Fixed checkSRTMgrids for positives values of lat/long.
* Small improvement in waterWeatherStation plot. Now the time axis is always 
  complete.
* Added daily summary for WeatherStation objects
* Fixed minor bug in vignette

VERSION 0.5

* Added maxit parameter to calcH to control the maximun number of iterations.
* Added an optional constrain to the selection of anchors pixels using the 
  location of the weather station and a 30km buffer.
* Changed default value for Z.om.ws in calcH. (From 0.0018 to 0.03)
* Added a new parameter to calcAnchors, available for both methods: buffer.
  buffer allow to set the minimun distance between two anchor pixels of the same
  kind
* Added a new method for calcAnchors = "CITRA-MCBbc". This method chooses the 
  coldest and hottest anchors pixels availables. Previous method ("CITRA-MCBr")
  chooses random pixel who meets the conditions. CITRA-MCBbc is now the default
  method for calcAnchors
* General remote sensing functions moved to a separate file
* loadImage detects when there is more than 1 image on the working folder
* Rn, G, H, LE are restricted to values > 0
* Improvements to anchors pixels selection: more releaxed hot 
  temperature criterium, distance, mean of many pixels, etc
* Added two methods for land surface estimation: single channel and split windows.
  Split windows only works for Landsat 8.
* loadImage now loads thermal data also: low gain for L7 and both bands for L8
* Fixed big bug when estimating ETo with a large weather station file

VERSION 0.3.2

* Solved errors when using non ASCII characters
* Fixes some bug who prevent using water with L8 data
* More flexibility on band names and changes to reflect the changes on USGS's 
  ESPA file naming
* Improvement to plot.waterWeatherStation to plot only data from the sattelite
  overpass when the object was created using a MTL file.

VERSION 0.3

* First release to CRAN!

VERSION 0.2.9

* Added dailyET function to calc and sum hourly ET for a complete day
* Added new parameter to hourlyET and dailyET to choose between ETo or ETr
* read.WSdata doesn't require a Landsat metadata anymore. Now you can process
  weatherstation data without a landsat flyby. print method changed to reflect
  this
* function METRIC.EB now accepts many parameters for method of the included 
  functions. For example, you can set with method you choose for LAI estimation
* We're approaching CRAN launch date. Changed version numbering to reflect this. 
  First CRAN version will be 0.3

VERSION 0.0.2.4

* Added function to prepare data from Weather Station and calc weather
  conditions at satellite flyby.
* Added function to calculate Surface Energy Balance using METRIC

VERSION 0.0.2.3

* Rename sensibleHeatFlux() to sensibleHeatFlux_old()
* Remove sensibleHeatFlux.CITRA()
* Split sensibleHeatFlux in 2 new functions:
  - calcAnchors() for anchors points
  - calcH() for the iterative process to calculate H and dT
* Added function to estimate Net Radiation
